The last one is the official norm in Finland and in Finnish, and I
suppose in Swedish as well.  The norm states that when listing many
decimal numbers which should be separated by commas, one could use a
semicolon instead of the list comma.  Eg. "24,3; 0,3784 ja 7,411".
("Ja" means 'and'.)

I was required to use a decimal point instead of a decimal comma--but
still no dots nor commas to separate numbers into three-figure
groups--when writing my thesis.  That's a custom at least at this
department of chemistry...

